{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>But as for me</b> — He recounts his good deed: 'when they were sick, my clothing was sackcloth.' Now we know that every affliction joined with the soul is always a fast: 'and you shall afflict your souls' (Leviticus 16:29), 'the afflicted soul shall satisfy' (Isaiah 58:5), 'a day when a man afflicts his soul' (Isaiah 58:5). If so, what is the reason to add the word בצום, and the bet is in construct state? The reason: on the day when his illness was heavy upon him, his friends and companions called a fast, and it was a known day; I too afflicted my soul on it." "<b>And my prayer shall return upon my bosom</b> — Rabbi Levi said: the meaning is that I was praying and bowing, and it was thrust back. The correct interpretation: in the manner of 'and restore to our neighbors sevenfold into their bosom' (Psalm 79:12) — that HaShem will give to me like my prayer and my request from HaShem on his behalf."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:29:40.040485699Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}